The software below is not part of this directory. It is what this directory
runs on, installed separately, with its own licence.

1. The OpenBilliards kernel (the pool_py module, built from crates/pool-py)

   MIT License. Parts of the physics kernel are derived from pooltool
   (https://github.com/ekiefl/pooltool), Copyright (c) Evan Kiefl and
   contributors, and remain under the Apache License 2.0: the NOTICE at the
   root of the repository carries the attribution, THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md
   lists the derived files, and web/vendor/LICENSE-Apache-2.0.txt holds the
   licence text. ACM's software terms may also apply to
   crates/pool-core/src/math/quartic.rs and crates/pool-core/src/math/cubic.rs,
   which implement ACM Algorithm 1010, and to the kernel built from them.

   No kernel binary comes with this directory: you build the pool_py module
   from the repository's source yourself (README.md, Install). Besides the
   kernel's own crates, its direct Rust dependencies, as
   crates/pool-py/Cargo.toml names them, are pyo3 (MIT OR Apache-2.0), numpy
   from the rust-numpy project (BSD-2-Clause) and serde_json (MIT OR
   Apache-2.0). The crates linked into the module with them, at the versions
   Cargo.lock pins, are under MIT, MIT OR Apache-2.0, or Unlicense OR MIT, and
   the module links the Rust standard library (MIT OR Apache-2.0).

   web/vendor/LICENSE-rust-crates.txt covers the repository's WebAssembly
   binaries only: it holds, verbatim, the notices of the Rust crates compiled
   into them, and does not cover this module.

2. Play-time dependencies (pyproject.toml, installed by pip)

   numpy 2.5.1: BSD-3-Clause AND 0BSD AND MIT AND Zlib AND CC0-1.0. Its
   wheels for some platforms also bundle OpenBLAS (BSD-3-Clause), with LAPACK
   inside it (BSD-3-Clause-Open-MPI), the GCC runtime libraries libgfortran
   and libgcc (GPL-3.0-or-later WITH GCC-exception-3.1) and libquadmath
   (LGPL-2.1-or-later).

   scipy 1.18.0: BSD-3-Clause for SciPy's own code. The code SciPy bundles
   keeps its own licence, and SciPy lists each one in LICENSES_bundled.txt in
   its source; the 1.18.0 wheels contain, among others, Qhull (the Qhull
   licence), Boost.Math (BSL-1.0) and HiGHS (MIT). Used at play time: the
   scrambled Sobol stroke pool comes from scipy.stats.qmc.Sobol. Its wheels
   also bundle runtime libraries that depend on the platform: OpenBLAS
   (BSD-3-Clause), with LAPACK inside it (BSD-3-Clause-Open-MPI); the GCC
   runtime libraries libgfortran and libgcc (GPL-3.0-or-later WITH
   GCC-exception-3.1); and libquadmath (LGPL-2.1-or-later). The macOS 14 arm64
   wheel bundles libgfortran, libgcc_s and libquadmath and takes BLAS and
   LAPACK from Apple's Accelerate. The Sobol direction numbers of S. Joe and
   F. Y. Kuo ship inside scipy; this directory vendors no direction table.

3. Training extras (pip install with the "train" extra; not needed to play or
   to evaluate the learned files)

   torch 2.13.0: Apache-2.0 AND Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception AND
   BSD-2-Clause AND BSD-3-Clause AND BSL-1.0 AND MIT (its wheel's
   License-Expression). It installs filelock (MIT in 3.32.0, the release
   checked here; torch does not pin it, and older filelock releases were
   under the Unlicense), typing-extensions (PSF-2.0), setuptools (MIT), sympy
   (BSD-3-Clause, with its LaTeX parser files under MIT) with mpmath
   (BSD-3-Clause), networkx (BSD-3-Clause), jinja2 (BSD-3-Clause) with
   markupsafe (BSD-3-Clause) and fsspec (BSD-3-Clause). On Linux, torch
   2.13.0 from the default package index also installs NVIDIA's CUDA
   libraries (cuda-toolkit components, cuda-bindings, nvidia-cudnn-cu13,
   nvidia-cusparselt-cu13, nvidia-nccl-cu13, nvidia-nvshmem-cu13) and triton,
   which come under their own licence terms.

   scikit-learn 1.7.2: BSD-3-Clause. It installs joblib (BSD-3-Clause),
   threadpoolctl (BSD-3-Clause) and scipy (above). Its macOS arm64 wheel
   bundles the LLVM OpenMP runtime, libomp (Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception).
